% §16.11 — naming a query where it is asked (§5/§14).
% Run by tests/system.rs::named_query_program_publishes_its_own_relation.
% The queries are the subject, so they live in the file.
person("alice", 34).
person("bob", 17).
person("carol", 29).
banned("carol").

% The hazard, unchanged: these are real `person` facts over a *subset* of
% `person`, and nothing downstream can tell them from the whole relation.
?- person(N, A), A >= 18.

% The fix — the same question, published under a name no source relation wears.
% Exact sugar for `adult(N, A) :- person(N, A), A >= 18.` plus `?- adult(N, A).`
?- adult: person(N, A), A >= 18.

% A name also replaces the synthesized `answer/N` the aggregate would otherwise
% force, `C` being bound by no atom.
?- with_bans: person(N, A), C = count { X | banned(X) }.

% No answer variables, so there are no columns to publish and the yes carries an
% argument instead — §5 bans 0-arity atoms, exactly as for `holds(true).`
?- clean: not banned("bob").

% The name is a *definition*, not a print-time label, which is what makes the
% answer compose: a rule may read the relation a query named.
eligible(N) :- adult(N, _), not banned(N).
?- eligible(N).
